From: Matthieu Baerts (NGI0) <matttbe@kernel.org>
Subject: [PATCH] mptcp: don't resend data acked without MPTCP opts

The previous commit resets the subflow, instead of resending data that
has been acked without MPTCP options, but only if this data had already
been resent. Data acked without these options the first time was still
resent once: a peer that had fallen back to TCP alone, e.g. because a
middlebox removed the DSS of new data after a zero window probe,
delivered it a second time to the application, without error.

Track the TCP sequence number acked with MPTCP options, to also catch
data acked without them the first time. Only before any MP_JOIN subflow
has been established, also for the data already resent: after that, the
peer can no longer fall back to TCP, and the Data ACK can be omitted
when its value does not advance, see RFC 8684, sections 3.7 and 3.3.2.
On a single subflow, a peer omitting the Data ACK like this can then see
its subflow reset if this Data ACK is lost, as before at the next
resend, or if it drops data after having acked it at the TCP level.

This issue has been reported by Clashiko, and reproduced with a new
Packetdrill test: data acked without MPTCP options was resent at the
MPTCP level, instead of a reset:

  dss_resend_after_bare_ack_reset_server.pkt:28: error handling packet: live packet payload: expected 0 bytes vs actual 1000 bytes

Two other new tests check that this data is still resent after a join,
but not after a join rejected by the peer.

Fixes: 119d51e225fe ("mptcp: fallback when MPTCP opts are dropped after 1st data")
Signed-off-by: Matthieu Baerts (NGI0) <matttbe@kernel.org>
---
v2: new, for an issue spotted by Clashiko
v4: commit msg: no more reset after a join for the data already resent
    either (Clashiko)

Note (Clashiko on v3): with a passive join processed while the MPTCP
socket is owned, the reset is only disabled at release time: a reset in
between needs this socket to be owned for an RTT, and a peer omitting
the Data ACKs right after the join.

Note (review): when the peer's MPTCP socket is owned at the third ACK of
the join, this peer can still fall back before finishing the join, and
then reject it: data acked without MPTCP options is then resent without
reset. A narrow race, needing the options to disappear at this moment.
